 "meta": {"name": "Fujicolor Superia Premium 400", "maker": "Fujifilm", "kind": "colour-negative", "cine": false, "iso": 400, "balance": "daylight", "log_h_ref": null, "densitometry": "Status M equivalent", "process": "CN-16", "grain": {"rms": 4}, "print_on": null, "document": "013AR0324A", "date": "2009-03", "notes": ["Fujifilm product information bulletin (Japanese), page 5 plots; reference number 013AR0324A, date read from the print code 神Q-09.03-FP. Film sold as Superia Premium 400.", "Characteristic curves: daylight exposure 1/125 s, process CN-16 (C-41 also usable), Status M equivalent. x axis is log H in lux-seconds as printed; b/g/r are three solid vector curves (blue on top, red lowest) drawn from log H -3.6 to 0.2 only.", "Spectral sensitivity: the sheet has no numbers on the y axis, only a scale arrow marking 1.0 (log unit) between two horizontal rules; y is therefore relative log sensitivity with 0 set at the lower rule and 1.0 at the upper rule (absolute offset not printed). Sensitivity is the reciprocal of the exposure (J/cm2) needed for a specified density (minimum density + 1.0); each layer curve ends in near-vertical drops (b about 470-505 nm, g about 465-600 nm, r about 567-678 nm), so the last few nm are approximated on the 5 nm grid.", "Spectral dye density plot has only 'mid' (中間濃度, density given by a neutral subject) and 'min' (最小濃度, minimum density), no separate c/m/y dyes; the curves run 400-720 nm. The page contains the same two paths drawn twice.", "Grain: diffuse RMS granularity 4 (printed as '……4' next to heading 18); micro-densitometer aperture 48 um, sample density minimum density + 1.0. Film speed ISO 400/27 deg daylight (tungsten 3200 K: ISO 100 with LBB-12). No paper is recommended on the sheet."]}}